      "year": 1997,
      "abstract": "Andre Liebich. From the Other Shore: Russian Social Democracy after 1921. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1997. xii, 476 pp. Illustrations. Notes. Sources. Index. $48.00, cloth. Andre Liebich begins and ends this meticulous biography of Menshevism by insisting, not without some warnings, on the utility of studying exile movements. His introduction deplores the lack of historical imagination which has brought scholars overwhelmingly to concentrate upon the winners, ignoring those who, by the fact of their exile, embody lost causes, defeat, rejection. Liebich's interest in exile movements generally prompts him to address this phenomenon. A second interest, equally compelling, is Liebich's concern with the fate of Marxism following the Russian Revolution. Together these bring him to consider Menshevism, above all in its decades of exile from its native land. Have the Mensheviks anything worthwhile to tell us? In addressing this question, Liebich stresses that his book is both a study of a political movement and a collective biography. One of the author's chief accomplishments is that he maintains a tight control over each of his two narratives, weaving them together into a consistent, complementary analysis. The first 95 pages cover the years in Russia, the remaining 250 pages the Menshevik exile in Berlin, Paris, and New York. During all these years, at home and abroad, there is enough in Liebich's narrative to reinforce a widespread conclusion about Bolshevism's Russian foes: that the Soviet regime was lucky in its enemies. Not that the Mensheviks set out to be an enemy. The celebrated \"Martov Line,\" destined to be the \"guiding tenet of the Menshevik party for the next twenty years,\" laid down that party members must not take part in any attempt to overthrow the Bolsheviks by force. However, this insistence depended on two ever shakier assumptions. The first was that no matter how distasteful the regime might be, it still commanded the support of substantial numbers of the working class who, misguided though they undoubtedly were, continued to see it as their government, defending their class interests. Furthermore, any party that replaced the Bolsheviks would be much worse and, indeed, counterrevolutionary. Baldly therefore, Martov concluded that Mensheviks should not be afraid to say that they opposed the \"democracy\" (in quotations) of the antiBolsheviks and they supported the tyranny (no quotations) of Lenin and Trotsky. In brief, the \"Martov Line\" expressed its creator's fervent wish that he and his party colleagues serve as a loyal opposition to a regime which recognized no such political possibility and took brutally effective measures to ensure that none should emerge. Well before their expulsion from Soviet Russia, Mensheviks could see their star dipping below the horizon. Their dismal showing in the Constituent Assembly election results sealed their political failure inside Russia: was their record any more positive, for themselves and democratic Russian socialism, during the decades abroad? Liebich's history of the party's responses to successive stages of Soviet development enables us to come to our own conclusions on this crucial point. \u2026",
      "citationCount": 12,
